Sure, let’s say that you wanted to ask people some questions and then repeat those questions until they answer "(YES)" to indicate that they’ve completed the survey.  In this case, the number of questions you want to ask is known in advance, so a "for" loop would be more useful than a "while" loop.  Here’s some sample code that uses a "for" loop to repeat the questions until someone says "(YES)":

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    printf("Question %d: ", i + 1);
    fflush(stdout);
    scanf("%d", &answer);
    if (answer == 1) {
        printf("OK, thank you for completing the survey.\n");
        return 0;
    }
}

This loop asks three questions, and repeats until someone says "(YES)".  The "if statement" inside the loop will execute only if the person answers "(YES)".

